WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.240
Why is NP closed under

2
00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.480
some operations while complement closure

3
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.620
remains unresolved? Certificates can record

4
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.280
a successful choice, a pair

5
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.000
of witnesses or a factorization.

6
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.780
We will build those witnesses

7
00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.920
explicitly and show why flipping

8
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.640
nondeterministic outcomes does not turn

9
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.220
an existential claim into its

10
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:19.700
negation.

11
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.280
For the union of two

12
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.240
NP languages, give a tag

13
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.960
naming the chosen language together

14
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.240
with its certificate. The verifier

15
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:28.520
checks the tag and runs

16
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.860
the corresponding verifier. A member

17
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.540
has a successful choice, while

18
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.280
a word outside both languages

19
00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:35.880
cannot pass either choice.

20
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.880
For intersection, give both certificates

21
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.440
and run both verifiers. The

22
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.840
combined verifier returns yes only

23
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.620
when both checks return yes,

24
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:47.640
so an input missing from

25
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.100
either language cannot pass. The

26
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.920
sum of two polynomial certificate

27
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.300
lengths and two polynomial runtimes

28
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.760
remains polynomial.

29
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.160
For concatenation, guess a split

30
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.780
index and give a certificate

31
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.000
for each side. Verify that

32
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.660
the first prefix belongs to

33
00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:05.940
the first language and the

34
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:07.520
remaining suffix to the second.

35
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.440
The split index is short,

36
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.440
and both substrings have length

37
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:13.040
at most the original input,

38
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.400
keeping the combined bound polynomial.

39
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.480
For star, a nonempty word

40
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.280
needs at most n nonempty

41
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.840
factors, where n is its

42
00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.080
length. Give their boundary indices

43
00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.600
and a certificate for each

44
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.960
factor, then verify every factor

45
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.200
using the original verifier. Remove

46
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.080
empty factors from a successful

47
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.620
decomposition; accept epsilon separately as

48
00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:37.720
the zero-factor case.

49
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.140
The bound on total witness

50
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.780
size matters here. Polynomially many

51
00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.140
pieces each have polynomially bounded

52
00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.060
certificates relative to a substring

53
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.300
of length at most n.

54
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.060
Their sum is therefore polynomial,

55
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.740
whereas guessing an unbounded number

56
00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.760
of factors without justification would

57
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:56.280
leave a gap.

58
00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.800
Now examine the false complement

59
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.760
proof that swaps every accepting

60
00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.400
and rejecting NTM outcome. The

61
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.320
original machine accepts when some

62
00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.480
branch accepts. After the swap,

63
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.100
the new machine accepts when

64
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.100
some original branch rejects, which

65
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.000
is not the same as

66
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.560
every original branch rejecting.

67
00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.000
For a concrete failure, let

68
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.900
one branch accept and two

69
00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:19.420
reject on the same input.

70
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.580
The original accepts because of

71
00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.260
its accepting branch, and after

72
00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.880
the swap two branches accept,

73
00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.080
so the swapped machine accepts

74
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.400
too. A genuine complement should

75
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.300
reject that original yes input.

76
00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.900
The class coNP consists of

77
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.120
complements of NP languages, not

78
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.620
the universe of languages with

79
00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.240
NP removed as a set

80
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.180
difference. Whether NP equals coNP

81
00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.460
is unresolved in the course

82
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.620
framework. The failed flip argument

83
00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:47.960
proves neither equality nor inequality;

84
00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:49.980
it merely fails to establish

85
00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:50.560
closure.

86
00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.480
Things to take note of.

87
00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.320
Give explicit polynomial-size certificates for

88
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.420
positive closure and include epsilon

89
00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.460
and empty-factor handling. Keep all

90
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.080
nondeterministic branches time-bounded, distinguish existential

91
00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.860
from universal branch claims, and

92
00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:05.860
do not turn the failure

93
00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.300
of one construction into an

94
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:08.740
unproved class separation.
